    "gearArmorRoleUNPOWERED": "Unpowered",
    "gearArmorTraditionHEGEMONY": "Hegemony",
    "gearArmorTraditionINDEPENDENT": "Independent",
    "gearArmorTraditionKNIGHTS_OF_LUDD": "Knights of Ludd",
    "gearArmorTraditionLIONS_GUARD": "Lion's Guard",
    "gearArmorTraditionLUDDIC_CHURCH": "Luddic Church",
    "gearArmorTraditionLUDDIC_PATH": "Luddic Path",
    "gearArmorTraditionPERSEAN": "Persean League",
    "gearArmorTraditionPIRATES": "Pirates",
    "gearArmorTraditionSINDRIAN_DIKTAT": "Sindrian Diktat",
    "gearArmorTraditionTRITACHYON": "Tri-Tachyon",
    "gearCatalogArmorAegisCompositeDescription": "Tri-Tachyon's Aegis exchanges thick conventional plate for laminated composites, predictive threat displays, and powered balance correction. It cannot absorb a slugging match like Legionary armor, but it moves quickly and presents a difficult firing solution.",
    "gearCatalogArmorAegisCompositeDisplayName": "Aegis composite line suit",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestDescription": "The Hegemony's answer to carrying the heavy thing: a powered suit built around the rack rather than a suit with a rack added to it. It is slower than a Legionary and worth every second of that, because the section walking with it can ask for indirect fire and get it from inside the room.",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestDisplayName": "Arbalest support battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorBulwarkHeavyDescription": "The League's Bulwark program favors modular plate blocks, replaceable actuators, and control standards that member worlds can reproduce. It lacks any one spectacular advantage and instead preserves credible protection, handling, and coalition repairability together.",
    "gearCatalogArmorBulwarkHeavyDisplayName": "Bulwark coalition battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchLineLore": "Palatine shells are inherited tools under discipline, not casual celebrations of machinery. Artisan-fitted ceramic and conservative joint governors make the formation slow, but its unusually resistant plate rewards the guardians who hold a chosen line.",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchLineProvenance": "Luddic Church sanctioned guard",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchWardensLore": "Parish wardens do not waste inherited machinery where common plate will serve. Ward kits protect the congregation’s ordinary defenders while two maintained Legionary shells per squad are entrusted to proven custodians expected to stand against the first breach.",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchWardensProvenance": "Luddic Church wardens",
    "gearCatalogArmorCombatDescription": "Kazeron's Bastion pattern lets League member worlds replace one another's plates and seals. Layered laminate trades modest mobility for dependable coalition line protection.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCombatDisplayName": "Bastion line armor",
    "gearCatalogArmorCordonShellDescription": "Cordon shells are obsolete station-security cuirasses sold after their seals, shock liners, or liability tags fail inspection. A local armorer can make one wearable again, but mismatched plates and tired joints leave the user with protection no professional force would call dependable.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCordonShellDisplayName": "Cordon surplus shell",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateHeavyLore": "Specter battlesuits make a walking tank behave like corporate response equipment: high-output joints, composite protection, and continuous threat prediction over maximum plate. They cross danger quickly and remain difficult to settle sights on, but cannot trade punishment with XIV shells forever.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateHeavyProvenance": "Tri-Tachyon blacksite security",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateLineLore": "Aegis composite armor refuses the low-tech premise that line infantry must become easy targets to survive. Predictive threat displays and powered balance correction preserve speed and reduce clean hostile solutions, paid for with less plate and a demanding maintenance train.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateLineProvenance": "Tri-Tachyon response teams",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorpsmanDescription": "Hegemony line establishments budget one trained corpsman per section and equip them properly: a powered harness that carries a surgeon's worth of consumables, keeps them cold, and leaves both hands free. The Navy's own figure for what it buys is a third of the casualties it would otherwise ship home.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorpsmanDisplayName": "Corpsman field rig",
    "gearCatalogArmorFieldFatiguesDescription": "Domain-pattern vac fatigues remain the working uniform of crews, militias, and cash-poor infantry. Local factories preserve the seals; unrestricted movement comes at the price of dangerous exposure.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFieldFatiguesDisplayName": "Domain-pattern vac fatigues",
    "gearCatalogArmorFieldFatiguesLore": "The founding issue: twelve worn Cordon shells over field fatigues. Surplus plates provide basic protection, with no integral systems, scout sensors, assault shells, or support harnesses. New equipment specifications improve the issue and open those roles.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFleetCombatLore": "Hegemony protection tables privilege standardized, repairable plate over elegant integration. Legionary suits dominate the establishment and Bastion laminates fill the remaining powered billets, giving the whole squad the weight to take contact and keep its formation.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFleetCombatProvenance": "Hegemony line formations",
    "gearCatalogArmorFoundryBreakerDescription": "Foundry-breakers begin as cargo exoskeletons and end beneath welded ship plate, illicit servos, and whatever shock padding a raider cell can steal. The crude mass takes extraordinary punishment before coming apart, but offers poor resistance and nearly no tactical grace.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFoundryBreakerDisplayName": "Foundry-breaker industrial rig",
    "gearCatalogArmorFurnaceLineDescription": "Sindrian Furnace armor surrounds a conventional pressure frame with thick petrochemical laminate and conspicuous state fittings. It carries more sacrificial plate than its peers, while heat, weight, and an unsubtle silhouette punish prolonged maneuver.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFurnaceLineDisplayName": "Furnace state line suit",
    "gearCatalogArmorHeavyDescription": "The XIV heavy battlesuit is the Hegemony's Domain-spec answer to a breach: dense ceramic, powered bracing, and standardized hardpoints. It survives punishment at an obvious cost in speed.",
    "gearCatalogArmorHeavyDisplayName": "XIV heavy battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyAuxiliaryLore": "The Hegemony expects auxiliaries to hold formation even when the quartermaster cannot furnish a regular battalion. Ward security kits cover the section while one Legionary suit per fire team anchors the billets most likely to receive contact.",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyAuxiliaryProvenance": "Hegemony auxiliary formations",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyShockLore": "A complete XIV battlesuit establishment is the Hegemony’s answer to a breach that must become a held corridor. Dense Domain-spec ceramic and standardized powered bracing absorb sustained punishment, while the squad accepts a measured advance and an obvious target profile.",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyShockProvenance": "Hegemony XIV shock tables",
    "gearCatalogArmorKnightsHeavyLore": "Reliquary battlesuits are restored one shell at a time and entrusted only after strict trials of stewardship. Their dense fitted armor turns aside heavy fire better than most infantry systems, but immense mass and conservative governors demand patience from the knight inside.",
    "gearCatalogArmorKnightsHeavyProvenance": "Knights of Ludd consecrated custody",
    "gearCatalogArmorLashplateHarnessDescription": "A Lashplate harness is a cargo frame, pressure web, and slabs of whatever a breaker crew could drill and wire into place. The mass can catch fragments and weak rounds, but crude balance, exposed lines, and almost nonexistent material matching make it cheap protection for expendable hands.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLashplateHarnessDisplayName": "Lashplate salvage harness",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueHeavyLore": "Bulwark suits extend coalition interoperability into the assault role. Replaceable actuators and modular plate blocks give member worlds a heavy system they can actually sustain, producing credible protection and handling without relying on one inaccessible miracle component.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueHeavyProvenance": "Persean League breach formations",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueLineLore": "A full Bastion schedule is the League ideal: not identical national uniforms, but plates, seals, and powered joints that member worlds have agreed to replace. Its protection and handling stay deliberately balanced because coalition logistics punish brilliant exceptions.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueLineProvenance": "Persean League coalition line",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueMobileLore": "A coalition screen values common interfaces more than a single world’s parade standard. Imported Janus suits give half the formation speed and threat awareness; locally replaceable Ward kits keep the remainder useful when the imported weave needs a depot.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueMobileProvenance": "Persean League mobile guard",
    "gearCatalogArmorLineDescription": "The Hegemony's Legionary suit favors interchangeable plates, pressure integrity, and field-serviceable joints. It is heavier than scout gear and built for disciplined formations far from a depot.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLineDisplayName": "Legionary line suit",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsGuardHeavyLore": "The Lion’s Mantle is armor as state theater: oversized cooling loops, lavish support, thick laminate, and polished fittings visible across a refinery concourse. Its enormous reserve rewards a disciplined frontal advance while weight and spectacle surrender both speed and subtlety.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsGuardHeavyProvenance": "Lion’s Guard prestige issue",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsMantleDescription": "Lion's Mantle suits carry thick Sindrian laminates, oversized cooling loops, and enough polished state ornament to make concealment irrelevant. Privileged logistics buy an enormous armor reserve; weight and silhouette leave the operator dependent on a disciplined advance.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsMantleDisplayName": "Lion's Mantle guard battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorMilitiaDescription": "Ward is the frontier name for locally assembled torso plate, sealed helmet, and available impact layers. Stations, Church villages, and auxiliaries issue it to section leaders.",
    "gearCatalogArmorMilitiaDisplayName": "Ward security kit",
    "gearCatalogArmorOrderlyDescription": "Parish infirmaries have kitted out stretcher parties for three centuries and see no reason to stop because the stretcher party is now under fire. The harness carries dressings, plasma and a folding litter where a weapons carrier would stow ammunition, and it is issued to whichever member of the section can be trusted to keep using it while being shot at.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOrderlyDisplayName": "Orderly aid harness",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawDescription": "Blackforge rig means cut-down security armor, industrial exoframe, ship plate, and hand-fitted seals from pirate or Pather shops. No two suits agree; the better examples are quick and stubborn.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawDisplayName": "Blackforge patchwork rig",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawHeavyLore": "Foundry-breakers are cargo exoskeletons buried under stolen servos and welded ship plate. Crude mass lets them keep moving after cleaner suits would fail, but poor resistance, brutal handling, and a vast target profile make every assault a wager against concentrated fire.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawHeavyProvenance": "Pather and pirate foundry cells",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawLineLore": "A Reaver rig is what remains when a Blackforge frame survives enough raids to earn powered bracing and still more ship plate. Veteran crews get speed and an intimidating reserve of material, but mismatched layers provide poor resistance to a serious penetrator.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawLineProvenance": "Veteran outlaw warbands",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawRaidersLore": "Blackforge shops cut security shells and cargo frames down for fast violent work, then issue the results to almost everyone expected to reach the target. The final pair receive sealed fatigues, a weapon, and no illusion that recovery is part of the plan.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawRaidersProvenance": "Pirate and Pather raiders",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutriderDescription": "One League member world builds the shell, another the array, a third the endurance pack, and the coalition argues about the interfaces for a decade. What comes out is a scout suit a section can live in for a week, and that puts everything it sees on a net six navies can read.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutriderDisplayName": "Outrider deep-recon suit",
    "gearCatalogArmorPackframeDescription": "An unpowered cargo frame with the load arms cut short and a launch rail welded where a crate used to sit. Independent crews build them because a section that can put one rocket into a bunker is worth more than a section that cannot, and because the frame was already in the hold.",
    "gearCatalogArmorPackframeDisplayName": "Packframe support harness",
    "gearCatalogArmorPalatineDescription": "Church armorers rebuild Palatine suits from inherited pressure shells, hand-fitted ceramic, and carefully husbanded powered joints. The result is slow and laborious to maintain, but unusually resistant once a sanctioned guardian sets their feet.",
    "gearCatalogArmorPalatineDisplayName": "Palatine legacy line suit",
    "gearCatalogArmorPathfinderDescription": "The Hegemony does not buy corporate sensor suits; it strips a Legionary down to the frame and hangs a surveying array off it. The Pathfinder is heavier, louder, and far easier to fix than anything Tri-Tachyon sells a scout, and an auxiliary can be taught to run one in a fortnight.",
    "gearCatalogArmorPathfinderDisplayName": "Pathfinder recon suit",
    "gearCatalogArmorReaverDescription": "A Reaver rig is a Blackforge frame that survived long enough to acquire powered bracing and another generation of ship plate. It is fast for its bulk and can soak punishment, but poor material matching gives penetrators few reasons to stop.",
    "gearCatalogArmorReaverDisplayName": "Reaver reinforced rig",
    "gearCatalogArmorReconLore": "Janus scout suits equip the operators expected to see the enemy, survive the first exchange, and disappear before heavier forces arrive. Cheaper Ward backing leaves the expensive sensor weave concentrated where corporate doctrine expects the best return.",
    "gearCatalogArmorReconProvenance": "Tri-Tachyon security doctrine",
    "gearCatalogArmorRedoubtDescription": "Hegemony siege issue: Legionary plate with every weight limit taken off, because the marine wearing it is not expected to go anywhere. It is what holds a breach once the assault sections have made one, and it is miserable to walk any distance in.",
    "gearCatalogArmorRedoubtDisplayName": "Redoubt siege plate",
    "gearCatalogArmorReliquaryHeavyDescription": "Each Reliquary shell is an artisan-restored legacy machine entrusted only to a Knight who has passed the Church's trials of custody. Dense fitted armor turns aside heavy fire, but conservative governors and immense mass make the wearer deliberate rather than swift.",
    "gearCatalogArmorReliquaryHeavyDisplayName": "Reliquary consecrated battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorRiotShellDescription": "Corporate security surplus, sold by the container to anyone who will take it. It was built to walk a picket line into a crowd, so it has a real shield and almost no assist worth the name -- and it turns out a station corridor is not so different from a picket line.",
    "gearCatalogArmorRiotShellDisplayName": "Kestrel riot shell",
    "gearCatalogArmorScoutDescription": "Tri-Tachyon's Janus suit hides composite segments beneath a sensor hood and electronic-warfare weave. Corporate scouts prize its speed; gray-market users struggle with the interfaces.",
    "gearCatalogArmorScoutDisplayName": "Janus scout suit",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianLineLore": "Furnace armor turns petrochemical industry into visible state power. Thick sacrificial laminate gives every soldier a deep protection reserve, while heat, ordinary resistance materials, and a conspicuous silhouette make maneuver and incoming fire less forgiving.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianLineProvenance": "Sindrian regular formations",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianSecurityLore": "A mixed protection schedule descended from refinery guards and planetary auxiliaries. Imported Janus suits go to mobile response billets, Ward kits go where cameras are watching, and the rest receive sealed fatigues beneath a very visible uniform.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianSecurityProvenance": "Sindrian security",
    "gearCatalogSystemRecoveredBatteryVolleyDescription": "A rebuilt shoulder battery carrying two finite salvos. It waits for a sighted target at standoff and shares the section's firing cadence. Military issue restores the larger four-salvo reserve.",
    "equipmentTemplateStorySourceRoute": "Acquisition belongs to an authored story reward. Ordinary market, license, patron and recovery channels do not offer this technology.",
    "debugPowerSuitHeading": "Power-suit test technology",
    "debugPowerSuitLearningNote": "These developer actions permanently learn two armor patterns in this save. Fit them manually through Barracks > Equipment; current personnel and kit stay as issued.",
    "debugPowerSuitLearnRecovered": "Learn recovered suit test patterns",
    "debugPowerSuitLearnMilitary": "Learn military suit test patterns",
    "debugPowerSuitLearned": "Learned {0} new armor patterns. Issue the tactic sheet from its card in Barracks > Equipment.",
    "debugPowerSuitNoRoster": "Marine roster is unavailable; no technology learned.",
    "loadoutCorporateSpecterSectionName": "Tri-Tachyon Specter Section",
    "loadoutCorporateSpecterSectionDescription": "Two recovered Specter powered breachers in the assault seats, a scout, two support gunners in Aegis composite and seven riflemen in combat armor. A first, light taste of powered armor: ten of twelve marines stay in ordinary kit.",
    "gearCatalogCorporateSpecterSectionProvenance": "Tri-Tachyon field trial",
    "gearCatalogCorporateSpecterSectionLore": "Tri-Tachyon sells prediction before it sells plate, and its first offer is two suits in a section of ordinary marines. The recovered Specter frames are a tier below the military pattern, which keeps the price of entry reasonable. The card carries the whole section, scouts and riflemen included.",
    "loadoutHegemonyArbalestSectionName": "Hegemony Arbalest Section",
    "loadoutHegemonyArbalestSectionDescription": "Two recovered Arbalest powered support gunners carrying the section's missile pods, with a Pathfinder scout and ten riflemen in Line armor. The rest of the section stays in ordinary Hegemony kit.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsTrenchClearingProvenance": "Freeport security surplus",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsTrenchClearingLore": "What a security contractor issues the week somebody digs in across a road: the same rifles as always, four old rocket tubes for the guns at the ends of the line, and a crate of grenades for the trench itself. It will not take a line held by regulars, but it gives a dockside company a chance against one held by people like themselves.",
    "gearCatalogFreeportAssaultProvenance": "Independent militia contracts",
    "gearCatalogFreeportAssaultLore": "Freeport militias cannot buy assault plate, so they buy a slug that goes through a dugout instead. Two rail packs put their single shots into the position while ten marines in militia plate close on it; it is the cheapest section that reliably opens a defended line.",
    "gearCatalogHegemonyArbalestSectionProvenance": "Hegemony auxiliary salvage",
    "gearCatalogHegemonyArbalestSectionLore": "Auxiliary sections get the Hegemony's recovered fire-support frames long before they see its assault plate. Two suits give a section its battery volleys; everybody else keeps marching in the standard line armor.",
    "loadoutSalvageAssistedName": "Salvage-assisted Section",
    "loadoutSalvageAssistedDescription": "Four recovered power suits, two Specter breachers and two Arbalest support gunners, among a scout and seven riflemen in militia armor. The whole section comes with the card; the suits are a third of the squad.",
    "gearCatalogSalvageAssistedProvenance": "Company salvage doctrine",
    "gearCatalogSalvageAssistedLore": "Recovered powered frames are concentrated in the breach and support billets while militia-grade scouts and riflemen fill out the section. It is the last step before a company fields whole sections in powered armor.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterRecoveredDisplayName": "Recovered Specter battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterRecoveredDescription": "A rebuilt corporate breach shell with mismatched composite panels and tired actuators. Its patched armor still outlasts regular infantry kit, but reduced protection and a slower predictive breach cycle leave a clear step to military issue.",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestRecoveredDisplayName": "Recovered Arbalest battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestRecoveredDescription": "A salvaged support shell with replacement plate, a worn load-bearing frame and a two-salvo shoulder battery. Its limited ordnance and reduced armor reserve leave a clear step to the four-salvo military pattern.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterHeavyDescription": "The Specter is a corporate breach shell built around high-output joints, composite armor, and continuous threat prediction. It gives up the XIV pattern's raw staying power to cross exposed ground faster and remain unexpectedly elusive for a walking tank.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterHeavyDisplayName": "Specter composite battlesuit",
    "gearCatalogArmorVigilDescription": "Diktat state security orders these by the crate for work its own citizens are not supposed to hear about. It is a riot shell built up until it can take a rifle round rather than a thrown brick -- heavier than the pattern it descends from, lighter than anything the Lion's Guard would deign to wear, and issued to people expected to go through a door on a schedule.",
    "gearCatalogArmorVigilDisplayName": "Vigil suppression shell",
    "gearCatalogBulwarkDescription": "A League siege walker built the way the coalition builds everything: replaceable actuators, shared control standards, and a hull thick enough that its faults can be argued about at leisure. Member worlds assemble it under licence from Kazeron drawings, and enough sets have been sold, captured and rebuilt since that no two are quite the same machine. It shares its name with the coalition battlesuit, and its crews will tell you it shares the temperament.",
    "gearCatalogBulwarkRole": "Heavy line chassis",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechBastionAutocannonsDescription": "The League's interchangeable autocannon in its paired arm fit, sharing feed geometry and control standards with half the coalition's other hardware. Kazeron sells the drawings as readily as the guns, which is why member worlds and the people raiding them field the same weapon. Interoperability rather than performance is the point of it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechDemolitionCannonDescription": "The Foundry Breaker: a short mortar bored out of mining stock and hung where an arm gun should be, meant for walls rather than for people. Pirate and Pather foundry cells build them from whatever the last yard yielded, which is why no two share a bore. It carries very few shells and every one of them is spent on architecture.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechDualChaingunsDescription": "A matched pair of Domain-pattern rotary cannon hung off an arm assembly, the arrangement every arsenal reaches for when it wants a walker able to answer everything at once. The mechanism is old enough that no faction claims it and common enough that none of them guards it. Crews call the second barrel the argument settler.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechDualLinearCannonsDescription": "Twin magnetic accelerators in an arm assembly, a Tri-Tachyon design licensed to anyone who can supply the cells and keep the rails trued. The corporate yards treat the pattern as unglamorous work and sell the drawings freely. Everyone else treats a walker that can reach that far as worth the maintenance.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechDualMusterAutogunsDescription": "A paired fit of the Muster, the mass-market convoy gun assembled around commodity receivers and open feeds. Commercial worlds, militias and contractors buy them by the crate, and captured sets turn up wherever those convoys stopped. It is common-market hardware in a military mount and it does not pretend otherwise.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechDualPulseLasersDescription": "A paired emitter array from the same Tri-Tachyon security family as the Lancer, scaled up until it needs a walker's power plant to matter. Corporate response teams field them; everyone else buys them second-hand and complains about the diagnostics. Nothing about the pattern is subtle, the light included.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechHeavyCannonDescription": "A single large-bore arm gun of Hegemony manufacture, built to put one deliberate shell into something that had earned it. It is the longest continuously produced mech weapon in circulation and the easiest anywhere to find ammunition for. Its crews are unhurried people.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLrm15Description": "The full artillery rack: boosted missiles on a shoulder mount, and the reason a support walker is built with its feet so far apart. Diktat and League batteries field them in pairs, and the drawings have leaked far enough that a pirate band occasionally manages one. A machine carrying two is not expected to be anywhere near the fighting.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLrm5Description": "A small indirect-fire rack that lobs its missiles over whatever stands in the way, using guidance a light chassis has no other use for. It is standard fire-support fitment across the coalition and the Diktat and circulates freely by salvage besides. Its crews rarely see what they hit.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechNoseChaingunDescription": "One of the same rotary cannon carried alone in a light chassis's nose, because a second would cost the machine a leg's worth of mass. Frontier yards fit them out of the crates the paired mounts arrive in. It is the cheapest way to make a raider worth taking seriously.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechNoseMusterAutogunDescription": "One Muster autogun in a light chassis's nose, which is how most militia walkers are armed and how most of them stay affordable. The same crates supply it as supply the paired mount, and the same yards fit it in an afternoon. Nobody has ever been proud of one.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechPioneerRocketDescription": "An open rail of seismic and obstacle-clearing rockets, adapted from public-works stock onto a shoulder hardpoint by yards with nothing better to hand. Frontier surveys, contractors and salvage crews all carry the pattern for the job it was drawn for. Bolted to a walker it is finite, loose and unsheltered — a cheaper answer than a sealed pod, and visibly one.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechQuarryBreakerDescription": "A mining and demolition projector on a walker's arm, sold out of extraction works and breaker yards to people who intend to knock something down. Its shell bin is small, its cycle is slow, and its accuracy is a matter of local opinion. Contractors and outlaws field it anyway, on the grounds that a wall is a large target.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechShoulderLaserDescription": "The Lance Cannon: a single heavy emitter on a shoulder yoke, drawing straight off the reactor and dispensing with ammunition altogether. Tri-Tachyon built the pattern for facilities that could not be resupplied; everyone else fits it to machines they mean to keep in the field indefinitely. Heat rather than supply is what limits it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechSrm15Description": "The heavy shoulder rack of the same sealed family, three cells deep and fed from a bin the chassis has to be designed around. Coalition and Hegemony formations issue them, wealthy independents buy them, and everyone else finds one still attached to something they had to kill first. It is a doorway weapon and it is unapologetic about it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechSrm5Description": "A small sealed short-range missile pod, the lightest rack a shoulder hardpoint will accept. Every arsenal in the sector builds a version, and the sealed cassette is what separates a military pod from a rail with rockets on it. A light chassis carries one and treats it as its whole argument up close.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechThermalLanceDescription": "Lion's Brand, a Sindrian prestige emitter fitted where a missile rack would go, bright enough that its crews are told to consider the effect on morale part of the weapon. Lion's Guard formations carry them, and diverted or captured examples are the only ones anybody else sees. It is expensive, conspicuous, and entirely serious.",
    "gearCatalogHoundDescription": "A light frame that began as a frontier survey walker and lost its instrument mast to somebody's cutting torch. Independent yards on a dozen worlds build the pattern out of commodity running gear, which is why it turns up in militia sheds, pirate holds and contractor manifests without anyone being able to say whose it was. It is quick, cheap to keep walking, and honest about how little it will absorb.",
    "gearCatalogHoundRole": "Light raider chassis",
    "gearCatalogSiroccoDescription": "A Sindrian-derived support walker laid out around a stable firing platform and two shoulder racks, exported in quantity because the Diktat would rather sell the machine than the doctrine for using it. The shoulders are its purpose; the arm mount exists so that the thing is not helpless once the racks run dry. Crews outside the Diktat learn its habits from salvage and from each other.",
    "gearCatalogSiroccoRole": "Fire support chassis",
    "gearCatalogSpecialAntiMaterielRifleDescription": "Tri-Tachyon's Breachlight drives a magnetic heavy round through armor seams without endangering nearby friendlies. It carries four shots, depends on proprietary cells, and cannot breach walls.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialAntiMaterielRifleDisplayName": "Breachlight Anti-Materiel Rifle",
    "gearCatalogSpecialBreachingCutterDescription": "Independent breaker yards cut hulls apart with the Emberjaw; Church custodians keep older units running and pirates convert whatever they salvage. The carrier must stand against the work and hold it: a long, visible channel that ruins armor and emplacements at arm's length, and opens an authored breach point. It has no blast and cannot chew arbitrary walls.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialBreachingCutterDisplayName": "Emberjaw Thermal Breacher",
    "gearCatalogSpecialBreachingCutterSubtitle": "Reusable cooldown-gated anti-hard contact channel",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldRevetmentDescription": "A Hegemony engineer pattern old enough that every arsenal builds its own: folding mesh baskets and a stake kit a marine drops across the front of the ground they are already holding. It stops nothing a soldier could not step over, and catches what crosses it at chest height. It covers one side of one boundary, and it comes down again.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldRevetmentDisplayName": "Rampart Field Revetment",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldRevetmentSubtitle": "Placed, self-expiring directional cover",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFragGrenadeDescription": "Kazeron adopted the Shattercap; leaked specifications now feed Diktat, pirate, and Pather arsenals. Its compact blast breaks exposed infantry but barely troubles walls or hardened emplacements.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFragGrenadeDisplayName": "Shattercap Fragmentation Grenades",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFragGrenadeSubtitle": "Limited-ammunition anti-personnel ordnance",
    "gearCatalogSpecialCommsRadioDescription": "A pack set and handset for a forward observer. While its carrier is alive, unsuppressed and sure of the ground, it lets them direct their side's fighters and bombers onto it. A strike over a strong defence is likely to meet anti-air.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialCommsRadioDisplayName": "Forward Observer Radio",
    "gearCatalogSpecialCommsRadioSubtitle": "Directs the air pool onto designated ground",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefenceEmplacementDescription": "A Gilead voidwright shipping-lane pod cut down for ground work: a cluster gun on a folding tripod that shreds incoming warheads and nothing else. It carries the rounds you brought, runs its cell flat, and can be shot like anything else standing in the open.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefenceEmplacementDisplayName": "Palisade Interceptor Pod",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefenceEmplacementSubtitle": "Placed, self-expiring ordnance denial",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefencePackDescription": "Hegemony issue for a squad that has to keep walking under shellfire: six interceptor missiles in an armoured launch block on a pack frame. It arms itself when warheads come in, and each interceptor climbs out of its tube, lights a second motor and runs onto the round where it will be. Close in, a mortar shell can land before one arrives, and six is not a barrage's worth; it thins the fire over a moving section rather than refusing it.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefencePackDisplayName": "Arrestor Interceptor Pack",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefencePackSubtitle": "Worn, self-arming ordnance cover for a moving squad",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldMortarDescription": "A two-piece infantry mortar of Hegemony pattern: a smoothbore tube, a baseplate and a bipod, carried by the gunner while a loader and a spotter carry the rounds and the eyes. It is set down where the section has halted, lobs high-angle shells onto ground it has been shown and cannot see, and is lifted again when the work is done or the enemy closes. It has a dead zone inside its minimum range and a small magazine, and a section that loses its gunner loses the tube.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldMortarDisplayName": "Anvil Field Mortar",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldMortarSubtitle": "Crew-served indirect fire, set down and lifted again",
    "gearCatalogSpecialRocketLauncherDescription": "The Hegemony cut an old Domain Annihilator cassette into a disposable shoulder cluster, then every low-tech arsenal copied it. It breaches walls and hardened emplacements without distinguishing friend from foe.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialRocketLauncherDisplayName": "Annihilator Rocket Launcher",
    "gearCatalogSpecialRocketLauncherSubtitle": "Limited-ammunition support weapon",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSatchelChargeDescription": "Gilead voidwrights built the Breachhand for sealed machinery and hull work; Knights maintain originals while Pathers and pirates copy them. Successful planting starts its recharge cycle.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSatchelChargeDisplayName": "Breachhand Mag-Clamp Kit",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSatchelChargeSubtitle": "Reusable cooldown-gated demolition utility",
    "gearCatalogSpecialScrapRocketDescription": "A workshop-made shoulder tube firing surplus demolition rockets. Poor sights, a slow reload and a weak shaped charge make it a threat to freight haulers rather than military armor.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialScrapRocketDisplayName": "Scrap-Tube Rocket",
    "gearCatalogSpecialScrapRocketSubtitle": "Two crude unguided rounds",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSmokeGrenadeDescription": "League quartermasters built Wayfarer smoke to defeat incompatible coalition optics. Its dense multispectral cloud harms no one, blocks both sides equally, and buys only seconds to move.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSmokeGrenadeDisplayName": "Wayfarer Smoke Grenades",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSmokeGrenadeSubtitle": "Limited-ammunition tactical utility",
    "gearCatalogSpecialVibroBladeDescription": "A rugged Hegemony boarding pattern copied across League and Diktat arsenals, with rare monofilament corporate tools and crude outlaw imitations beside it. One short reaction against somebody already inside arm's reach when a firefight collapses into contact. It cannot be used on machinery, and it will not chase anyone.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialVibroBladeDisplayName": "Quillon Vibro-Blade",
    "gearCatalogSpecialVibroBladeSubtitle": "Reusable cooldown-gated anti-personnel contact reaction",
    "gearCatalogSystemApertureReadDescription": "The hood's sensor cluster and the weave under the plate normally idle at whatever the licence permits. Held at the edge of ground the scout cannot see into, the suit spends its whole thermal budget on one wide active return and puts the result straight onto the company net: the room, the street, and a short way through the nearer walls, for as long as the emitters can stand it. It is proprietary, it is expensive to run, and it tells you what is there. It does nothing else.",
    "gearCatalogSystemApertureReadDisplayName": "Aperture read",
    "gearCatalogSystemAssaultBracingDescription": "Domain-spec bracing driven off the suit's own plant, on a doctrine timer rather than a gamble. It carries the wearer through the threshold at a pace the Hegemony can train, repeat, and account for, with the frontal plate locked forward the whole way. Nothing about it is improvised, which is the entire point.",
    "gearCatalogSystemAssaultBracingDisplayName": "Assault bracing",
    "gearCatalogSystemBatteryVolleyDescription": "Four salvos in the rack and a doctrine for spending them. Where the Aegis waits for a firing solution its own display likes, the Arbalest waits for a target its section commander would call worth a missile, and then sends more of them than the Aegis carries in total. It is not cleverer. It is a battery.",
    "gearCatalogSystemBatteryVolleyDisplayName": "Battery volley",
    "gearCatalogSystemBlazonAdvanceDescription": "The cooling loops dump everything they have been hoarding and the guard suit advances gleaming, plate spread wide. It is a magnificent thing to walk behind and an unmissable thing to shoot at, and the loops take a very long time to forgive it.",
    "gearCatalogSystemBlazonAdvanceDisplayName": "Blazon advance",
    "gearCatalogSystemBreacherAssistDescription": "Industrial shoulder rams and a salvaged riot screen, wired to the same trigger by somebody in a hurry. It fires hard enough to throw the rig through a doorway and no further, the screen is a sheet of scrap, and the servos need a long sulk afterwards. The flanks are exactly as exposed as they always were.",
    "gearCatalogSystemBreacherAssistDisplayName": "Breaching assist",
    "gearCatalogSystemCoalitionReadDescription": "The deepest active return anyone fields, and the only one built to be shared rather than owned. It opens the ground ahead, reads well past the nearer walls, and recovers quickly enough that an outrider can spend it twice crossing one street. What it costs is the pack, the weight, and the certainty that anybody listening heard it.",
    "gearCatalogSystemCoalitionReadDisplayName": "Coalition read",
    "gearCatalogSystemConsecratedAdvanceDescription": "The custody governors release and the shell walks forward behind its own fitted plate, unhurried, for a long time. A Knight in this suit is not crossing the room quickly. They are crossing it in front of somebody else.",
    "gearCatalogSystemConsecratedAdvanceDisplayName": "Consecrated advance",
    "gearCatalogSystemCorpsmanKitDescription": "The same act the parish orderly performs, done by somebody taught how and carrying enough to do it repeatedly. It reaches a little further, is worth putting on a marine who is merely hurt rather than nearly finished, and does not run out halfway through the fight it was brought for.",
    "gearCatalogSystemCorpsmanKitDisplayName": "Corpsman kit",
    "gearCatalogSystemInterlockAdvanceDescription": "Coalition breaching drill rendered in hardware: the actuators surge and the shoulder blocks interlock outward, covering a wider front than one wearer strictly needs. Member worlds standardized on the wider arc because the marine beside you may not be from your navy.",
    "gearCatalogSystemInterlockAdvanceDisplayName": "Interlock advance",
    "gearCatalogSystemLegionaryBraceDescription": "The suit's own answer to the problem the sandbag solves badly: the hip and knee actuators lock against each other into a frame the marine is standing inside rather than holding up, and the shoulder becomes a mount instead of a joint. Field-serviceable, like everything else the Hegemony issues, and expected to be serviced -- a brace is a mechanism, and a mechanism that has planted a thousand times knows it.",
    "gearCatalogSystemLegionaryBraceDisplayName": "Legionary brace",
    "gearCatalogSystemParishAidDescription": "Kneel down, find the hole, close it, and get them back on their feet. It is not sophisticated and it does not need to be: most of what puts a marine out of a fight is bleeding, and most of that is answered by somebody willing to stop and deal with it. The satchel holds what it holds.",
    "gearCatalogSystemParishAidDisplayName": "Parish aid",
    "gearCatalogSystemPredictiveBreachDescription": "The threat display picks the moment and the joints spend everything on it. The window is short and comes around again quickly; the screen is a courtesy rather than a wall. Corporate doctrine holds that the fastest way through a killing ground is to not be standing in it.",
    "gearCatalogSystemPredictiveBreachDisplayName": "Predictive breach",
    "gearCatalogSystemPredictiveVolleyDescription": "The same threat display that buys the Aegis its footing can just as easily lock a firing solution. When the wearer holds a clear line on something worth spending it on, the suit's shoulder pod puts a brace of smart micro-missiles into it before the wearer would otherwise get a shot off. The rack is small, it is not the wearer's rifle, and it does not refill in the field.",
    "gearCatalogSystemPredictiveVolleyDisplayName": "Predictive volley",
    "gearCatalogSystemRailShotDescription": "One missile, on a rail, aimed by the wearer turning to face the thing. There is no threat display and there is no second round: the frame carries what it was loaded with in the hold, and the crew decided which target was worth it before the drop. Often they decided wrong.",
    "gearCatalogSystemRailShotDisplayName": "Rail shot",
    "gearCatalogSystemRedoubtPlatformDescription": "Siege plate is the Legionary with the weight limits taken off, and the brace is what gets the most out of that: there is enough mass in the suit to plant against that the marine inside stops being a person aiming a weapon and becomes the ground the weapon is aimed from. It holds longer, recovers quicker, and costs proportionally less mobility than the lighter brace does, for the plain reason that there was less of it to give up.",
    "gearCatalogSystemRedoubtPlatformDisplayName": "Redoubt firing platform",
    "gearCatalogSystemRiotBraceDescription": "The shield comes up and the frame locks behind it. That is the whole system: no shove, no crossing speed, nothing a breach family would recognise as an assist. What it does have is a genuine shield, which covers a narrow slice of one direction and covers it better than a suit this cheap has any right to.",
    "gearCatalogSystemRiotBraceDisplayName": "Riot brace",
    "gearCatalogSystemSandbagRestDescription": "There is nothing in the vest that does this. What there is, is a section leader who has been told where the lane is, a wall or a bipod or a sack of ballast to lay the weapon across, and the patience to stay behind it. It is the cheapest version of the idea and the one every other version is a refinement of.",
    "gearCatalogSystemSandbagRestDisplayName": "Sandbagged rest",
    "gearCatalogSystemSuppressionBraceDescription": "A shield and a shove, both adequate, neither remarkable. It is the version of the idea a state can afford to issue to everybody, and it is worth exactly what the Diktat pays for it: enough to get a section through a doorway that a riot shell would not have survived, and nowhere near enough to make that pleasant.",
    "gearCatalogSystemSuppressionBraceDisplayName": "Suppression brace",
    "gearCatalogSystemSurveySweepDescription": "A mapping array asked to do something it was not sold for. It reads further than a Janus hood and holds the return longer, because it is bigger and the Hegemony does not mind that it announces itself doing so. Everyone within a few streets knows a survey sweep happened. Doctrine treats that as somebody else's problem.",
    "gearCatalogSystemSurveySweepDisplayName": "Survey sweep",
    "gearCatalogWaylineForwardScreenLore": "Wayline's convoy scouts learned to value a clear approach over another layer of plate. Six Milspec Lancers and six dependable Rooks equip a company that wants to see trouble early. Castle pairs the first issue with a scout-heavy armor plan; the weapon definition remains reusable with other plans.",
    "gearCatalogWaylineSteadyLineLore": "A quartermaster's thank-you after the checkpoint relief. Three carefully maintained Stalwart automatics give each fire team an anchor; nine familiar Rooks keep ammunition and replacement parts manageable for a small company. Castle teaches the first issue against a line-holding armor plan.",
    "gearCatalogWaylineSteadyLineProvenance": "Wayline cooperative",
